  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon an upper-level ridge builds in briefly over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring mostly \ndry weather with status quo temperatures to the area. Along the \nRocky Mountain Front this afternoon there will be snow mixed with \nrain at times and primarily all rain at lower elevations. This \nafternoon/evening there will be isolated rain showers along the Hi-\nLine. On Sunday a closed upper-level low will move over North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana with a 700mb low moving \nover southern Alberta. This will bring strong southerly flow aloft \nwhich will bring strong winds to north-south orientated valleys \nof Southwestern Montana. On Sunday along the higher peaks of the \nRocky Mountain Front there will be snow with the rest of the \nRocky Mountain Front seeing rain and/or a rain/snow mix. On Sunday\nacross the rest of North-central Montana a frontal boundary will \nmove from west to east and bring rain showers with embedded \nthunderstorms. Some of these rain showers/any thunderstorms have \nthe potential to produce gusty winds. See the Forecast Confidence \n& Scenarios section for details. On Sunday warm air advection will\nbring seasonable temperatures to Southwestern Montana with near \nnormal temperatures north of the I-90 corridor. \n\nOn Monday the upper-level low exits North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. Precipitation will linger across North-central \nMontana on Monday. On Monday low-level winds will increase which \nwill bring at least breezy conditions to North-central Montana. On\nMonday temperatures will remain cool. Tuesday through Thursday \nzonal flow with shortwave troughs moving through will affect \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow \ntemperatures to be slightly below seasonal averages with isolated\nshowers around. On Friday an upper-level ridge moves over North- \ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring above \nnormal temperatures with dry weather to the area. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nOn Sunday there is a 40 - 60% chance for sustained winds of 40 mph\nor greater and a 20 to 50% chance for wind gusts of 55 mph or \ngreater in the Madison River Valley. As a result a High Wind Watch\nremains in effect for the Madison River Valley from 12 PM to 9 \nPM. On Sunday there is greater than a 50% chance for 55 mph wind \ngusts along the ridgetops of the Madison and Gallatin Mountains. \nOn Monday there is around a 50% chance for wind gusts of 55 mph \nor greater across Judith Basin and Fergus Counties. Due to a lack\nof 700 mb support for strong winds for Judith Basin County, a \nHigh Wind Watch was only issued for Fergus County from 9 AM to 6 \nPM on Monday. \n\nThere is a 50 - 80% chance for a tenth of an inch of snow or \ngreater along the majority of the Rocky Mountain Front Sunday into\nMonday. There is currently a low chance that isolated mountain \npeaks will receive in excess of 3 inches of snowfall. Sunday into \nMonday there is a 30 - 60% chance for a quarter inch of \nprecipitation or greater across North-central Montana. Sunday \nevening a line of showers will move through North-central Montana.\nAs this line moves east of US Highway 87 between Great Falls and \nHavre there is a low chance for an embedded thunderstorm to \nproduce gusty to strong winds. If temperatures continue to trend \nupward on Sunday that will increase the instability and lead to a \ngreater risk for a thunderstorm to produce gusty to strong winds. \nOn Tuesday morning there is a 30 - 60% chance for low temperatures\nof 36 degrees or colder across the valleys of Southwestern \nMontana. -IG",
